---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { TOWNS } from '../../data/towns';
import { BUSINESS } from '../../data/business';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Areas we serve', path: '/locations/' }];
const quickAnswer = 'Trusted Termite and Pest Solutions works out of Seminole, Texas and covers roughly a hundred miles in every direction — Gaines, Yoakum, Andrews, Dawson, Terry, Howard, Midland, Ector and Lubbock counties. There is no trip fee, though job minimums are discussed for out-of-town work.';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Areas We Serve | Seminole &amp; the Permian Basin"
  description="Pest control, termite, lawn and junk removal across Gaines County and the Permian Basin, run out of Seminole. Thirteen towns on the route, no trip fee."
  path="/locations/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">
        {TOWNS.filter((t) => !t.byRequest).length} towns on the route, {TOWNS.filter((t) => t.byRequest).length} more by request · one truck, based in Seminole
      </p>
      <h1>Where we work</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1.8rem">
        {TOWNS.map(t => (
          <a class="card" href={`/locations/${t.slug}/`}>
            <h3>{t.name}, TX{t.byRequest ? ' — by request' : ''}</h3>
            <p>{t.county}{t.distanceMiApprox > 0 ? ` · about ${t.distanceMiApprox} miles ${t.direction} of Seminole` : ' · home market'}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="measure" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <p>
          The furthest we normally drive is about a hundred miles, which puts Lubbock and Big Spring at
          the edge of the map. If you are outside that and it matters to you, call and ask — sometimes
          it works out.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Two of those towns are marked "by request", and the distinction is real.</strong> The
          rest are places we actually run — there is a route, and it passes through. Stanton and Kermit
          are not on it. We have no customers in either, and nothing takes us through them on a set day.
          If somebody there calls with work that can be planned, we will drive it; that is a different
          promise from the one the other nine towns carry, and labelling them the same would be the
          easiest lie on this entire site to tell.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">The shape of the route</h2>
        <p>
          Seminole sits in the middle of a region that most maps flatten into one thing and that is
          actually three. Understanding which of the three your property is in explains more about your
          pest pressure than anything else on this site.
        </p>
        <p>
          To the north and north-west — Seagraves, Denver City, Brownfield, Lubbock — the land climbs onto
          the southern Llano Estacado. Flat, high, sandy, agricultural. Cotton and peanuts and grain, with
          irrigation circles laid across it and playa depressions that hold water after rain and are dry
          the rest of the year. This is the part of the route where the pest calendar is driven by farming
          activity: planting, irrigation, and above all harvest, which displaces rodents and field insects
          toward structures on a schedule you could nearly put a date on.
        </p>
        <p>
          To the south and south-east — Andrews, Midland, Odessa, Big Spring, Stanton — the country drops
          toward the Permian Basin proper. Caliche, mesquite, oilfield infrastructure, and a housing stock
          shaped by boom cycles: rapid build-outs, rental turnover, RV parks, and structures that sit
          empty for months and then fill in a week. Vacancy is a pest condition here in a way it is not
          further north, and scorpion pressure is the defining feature of the residential work.
        </p>
        <p>
          And in the middle — Gaines County itself, Seminole and Lamesa and the country around them — the
          two overlap. Agricultural land and oilfield activity sitting on the same sections, with a
          resident population that is genuinely rural: acreage, outbuildings, shops, barns, and properties
          where the house is the least interesting building on the lot.
        </p>
        <p>
          One truck covers all three, which is why the town pages read differently from each other. The
          technical work is the same everywhere. What is driving the problem is not.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">The counties</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Gaines County.</strong> Home county, Seminole the seat. The largest share of the work
          and the shortest drives. Agricultural, with a substantial rural residential population on
          acreage outside the city limits where county rules rather than city ordinances apply.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Yoakum County.</strong> Denver City and the country around it, north-west. Oil and
          agriculture together, small population, and very little pest control based locally.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Terry County.</strong> Brownfield, north. Cotton country on the caprock, an older
          housing stock in town, and the same harvest-driven rodent pattern as the rest of the Llano.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Dawson County.</strong> Lamesa, east. Agricultural, with a town centre that predates
          most of the region's oilfield build-out and a building stock that reflects it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Andrews County.</strong> Andrews, south. Notable on this route for having no
          full-service pest control company physically based in it, which shapes how service actually
          reaches properties there.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Martin County.</strong> <a href="/locations/stanton/">Stanton</a>, south-east, between
          Midland and Big Spring on the interstate — by request rather than on the route, and the page
          says so in the first paragraph.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Midland and Ector Counties.</strong> Midland and Odessa — the two largest markets on
          the route, the most competitive, and the ones with the most developed municipal enforcement
          around weeds and property condition.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Howard County.</strong> Big Spring, at the south-eastern edge of the map.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Lubbock County.</strong> Lubbock, at the northern edge — the largest city on the route
          and the furthest regular drive, run as planned days rather than on demand.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">City weed and property ordinances across the route</h2>
        <p>
          Almost every town on this list enforces a maximum vegetation height inside the city limits, and
          the numbers are not the same from town to town. People move between these towns constantly and
          assume the rule travels with them. It does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          The heights in force range from eight inches at the strict end to eighteen inches at the more
          permissive, with twelve inches common in the middle. Unincorporated county land works differently
          again: Health and Safety Code §343.011(c)(4) defines the county weed nuisance by proximity — within
          three hundred feet of another residence or commercial establishment — rather than by any height at
          all. The <a href="/compliance/weed-ordinances/">full comparison is set out here</a>, and the individual town pages carry the specifics for each place,
          including the notice periods and how abatement costs are recovered, because the details matter
          and getting them wrong is what turns a mowing job into a lien.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things are worth knowing wherever you are. First, the clock usually starts at the notice,
          not at the inspection, so the date printed on the paper is the date that matters. Second,
          several of these ordinances allow the city to issue a single notice per year and then abate
          repeat occurrences without notifying you again — which is how people end up with charges they
          did not see coming on a lot they thought was fine.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you have received a notice, read us the date when you call. It changes how the work is
          scheduled rather than being something to discover after the visit is booked.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">How distance actually works</h2>
        <p>
          There is no trip fee on this route. That is a deliberate choice and it is worth explaining what
          it does and does not mean.
        </p>
        <p>
          It means you will not see a mileage line on your invoice, and it means the price you were quoted
          on the phone is the price. It does not mean distance is free — on a one-truck operation it is
          one of the largest real costs in the business — and pretending otherwise would be the kind of
          claim this site exists to avoid. What it means is that distance is accounted for in how work is
          scheduled and, on larger jobs a long way out, in a job minimum discussed before you agree to
          anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          Practically, the route divides into two tiers. Seminole and the towns closest to it are
          responsive: a call in the morning can often become a visit the same day, and something urgent
          is genuinely urgent. The outer towns — Lubbock and Big Spring at the extremes, with Midland,
          Odessa, Lamesa and Brownfield in between — run as planned days, grouped so that several
          properties in one town are handled together.
        </p>
        <p>
          The consequence for you is simple and it is the single most useful thing on this page: notice
          buys you a better slot than urgency does. A call on Monday for work in Lubbock lands
          considerably better than a call on Thursday morning hoping for Thursday afternoon, and no amount
          of goodwill changes the seventy-five miles in between.
        </p>
        <p>
          Grouping also means that if you know your neighbours are looking for the same work — which on
          acreage and in small towns they very often are — mentioning it is worth doing. Several
          properties in one town on one day is cheaper to deliver and easier to schedule than the same
          properties spread across a month.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">Where the business is based, and why that is on the map</h2>
        <p>
          Seminole is not a marketing choice. The owner grew up here, graduated high school here and has
          been in and out of Gaines County his whole life, and the business is based here because this is
          where he lives.
        </p>
        <p>
          That matters for a practical reason rather than a sentimental one. A company headquartered in
          Midland or Odessa that lists Seminole as a service area is describing a place it drives to. The
          direction of travel on this route runs the other way, and the towns nearest Seminole — the ones
          large regional operators reach last and least often — are the ones served first and fastest here.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also why El Paso does not appear anywhere on this map despite six years of the owner's
          trade experience happening there. That is where he trained, not a place this company serves, and
          listing it as coverage would be exactly the kind of padding that makes service-area pages
          worthless.
        </p>
        <p>
          Being local also changes what a town page can honestly contain. Knowing which subdivisions were
          built in which decade, which streets hold water after a storm, and which parts of town sit
          against open field is not research — it is having lived here. Where that knowledge exists it is
          on the page. Where it does not yet, the page says less rather than sounding as though it does.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same rule governs the neighbourhood pages sitting beneath several of these towns. They exist
          where specific local knowledge supports them and they stay unpublished where it does not, which
          is why some towns already have several and others have none at all yet.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">Why the town pages are written the way they are</h2>
        <p>
          Most pest control websites in this region have a page for every town within a hundred miles,
          and most of those pages are the same page with the town name swapped. You can usually confirm
          it in under a minute: read two of them and look for a sentence that would be true of anywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pages linked above are not built that way, and the difference is deliberate. Each one is
          written from things that are specifically true of that place — the local vegetation ordinance
          and its actual numbers, what the housing stock is and roughly when it was built, what the
          surrounding land is doing, what the pest calendar looks like there, and how the drive from
          Seminole works in practice.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where a town has less verified local detail behind it, its page is shorter and says less rather
          than being padded out with generic material to match the others. That is a decision with a
          real cost in search terms and it is the right one, because a page that invents local specifics
          is worse than no page at all — it reads convincingly right up until a resident notices that the
          detail is wrong, and then everything else on the site is in question too.
        </p>
        <p>
          As more genuine local knowledge accumulates — ordinances confirmed by phone, neighbourhoods
          worked, patterns observed across a season — the thinner pages get deeper. That is the honest
          way for a regional surface to grow, and it is slower than the alternative.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">What is the same everywhere on this map</h2>
        <p>
          The town pages spend most of their length on differences, so it is worth setting out what does
          not change across the route, because it is the majority of the picture.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Scorpions.</strong> The defining residential pest of this whole region, from Lubbock to
          Big Spring, and the one that generates more calls in July than everything else combined. The
          approach does not vary by town: find the harbourage, close the entry, treat the routes between
          them. Properties differ enormously; the method does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Dryness as a driver.</strong> Everything out here is looking for moisture, and any
          reliable source is a destination. Irrigation heads throwing against a foundation, a condensate
          line discharging in the same spot all summer, a leaking spigot, a swamp cooler line. In wetter
          parts of the country these are minor maintenance issues. Here they are the single most reliable
          predictor of where the pest problem will be.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Wind and dust.</strong> Seals fail faster here. Door sweeps wear, weatherstripping
          hardens and cracks, screens tear, and the gaps that result are the ones that matter. A
          five-year-old sweep in this climate is not the same component it was when it was fitted, and
          replacing one is frequently worth more than a treatment.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Termites, contrary to reputation.</strong> Plenty of people out here have been told the
          climate is too dry for termites. It is not, and the assumption is why activity so often goes
          unnoticed until it is substantial. Subterranean termites work below grade where the moisture
          is, and slab construction hides the evidence rather than preventing the problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Autumn rodents.</strong> The first genuinely cold nights move mice and rats indoors
          across every town on this list, on much the same schedule, and the buildings that get hit are
          the ones whose gaps were there all along. Exclusion in September is worth more than trapping in
          December, everywhere.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">Rural properties and county land</h2>
        <p>
          A large share of the work on this route is not in any town. It is on acreage, on section roads,
          at addresses where the nearest neighbour is a quarter mile away and the nearest city limit is
          several miles further. That work behaves differently enough to be worth its own explanation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The buildings multiply.</strong> A rural property is rarely one structure. House, shop,
          barn, equipment shed, well house, hay storage, sometimes a second dwelling. Each one is its own
          inspection and its own set of entry points, and the population usually lives in the outbuildings
          and visits the house rather than the other way round. Counting your buildings before you call
          is the single most useful thing you can do for the accuracy of a quote.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The field is the boundary.</strong> Where a property abuts cultivated land, the field
          sets the calendar. Irrigation coming on, a crop maturing, and above all harvest each push
          populations toward whatever structures are nearest. Properties on the edge of a section see
          this as a sudden event rather than a gradual increase, and they see it at roughly the same time
          every year.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Feed and grain change everything.</strong> Livestock feed, bagged or in bins, spilled
          grain, hay — any of these is a permanent food source, and no treatment regime outcompetes a
          permanent food source. Where feed is present, storage and exclusion get discussed before product
          does, because the alternative is paying repeatedly for the same outcome.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The rules are different.</strong> Outside city limits, municipal weed and property
          ordinances do not apply. County-level standards are generally more permissive, and the
          enforcement mechanism is different. That is a relief for most rural owners and occasionally a
          surprise for someone who has just moved out from town and assumed the rules followed them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Access needs planning.</strong> Gates, dogs, cattle guards, locked shops, and the
          question of whether anyone will be on the property. None of these are obstacles if they are
          mentioned in advance. All of them are wasted drives if they are not, and on this route a wasted
          drive is an hour each way rather than ten minutes.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">Working across three markets at once</h2>
        <p>
          There is a version of a regional service page that claims equal expertise everywhere and means
          it nowhere. This is not that, so here is the honest distribution.
        </p>
        <p>
          Seminole and Gaines County are the home market, the deepest knowledge and the fastest response.
          That is where the operator grew up, where the business is based, and where the property-level
          familiarity — which neighbourhoods have which construction, which streets flood, which
          subdivisions were built in which decade — is genuinely specific rather than generic.
        </p>
        <p>
          The towns in the middle ring are worked regularly and known well: the ordinances, the housing
          stock, the seasonal patterns, the parts of town where the problems concentrate. These are
          planned-day markets rather than same-day, and the work there is steady rather than occasional.
        </p>
        <p>
          The two large cities and the far edges are real service areas, worked on scheduled runs, with
          the honest caveat that a one-truck operation covering a hundred-mile radius is not going to be
          the fastest option in a city that has a dozen companies inside its own limits. What it can be
          is the one where the owner answers the phone, the licence holder does the work, and nothing is
          relayed through a call centre. For a lot of people that trade is worth making. For someone who
          needs a technician within two hours on a Tuesday, it may not be, and that is a fair thing to
          weigh.
        </p>

        <h2 style="margin-top:2.6rem">If your town is not on the list</h2>
        <p>
          The map has edges but they are not walls. There are properties between these towns, on ranch
          roads and county roads and at addresses that do not belong to any municipality, and they are
          served the same as anywhere else — usually on the day the truck is already heading that
          direction.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are somewhere not named above and within roughly a hundred miles of Seminole, call and
          ask. The answer depends on the day and on what else is scheduled nearby, and it is a thirty
          second conversation rather than a formal enquiry.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are well outside that, the honest answer is that we are not the right call, and you will
          be told so directly rather than strung along toward a visit that was never going to work. There
          are good operators in most parts of this state, and sending you to one is a better outcome than
          driving three hours to a job that cannot be priced sensibly.
        </p>
        <p>
          One thing this site deliberately does not do is list towns it does not serve in order to appear
          in more searches. Every town named on this page is a place work actually gets done. That is a
          lower number than most pest control websites in this region advertise, and it is the accurate
          one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Cta />
</BaseLayout>
